  <prompt>
    Make the three-pane workspace COORDINATE for real, and prove it with a real-PaneEventBus cross-pane
    test. Today the R2 differentiator is stub-only despite task 070 being open and Flows 1&2 being "wired":
    4 of 6 compose flows are stub/contract-only; the Flow 1&2 receivers are LOG-ONLY and sit on the WRONG
    pane (ComposeWorkspace, not the Context/Assistant panes that should react); only Flow 5 is genuinely
    wired; and none of the 6 `compose_*` discriminants exist in the typed bus union — they all ride via
    `as any`, an ADR-030 violation. Close register Cluster 5 gaps 5.1–5.2: put REAL receivers on the
    Context and Assistant panes, build Flows 3/4/6, and add the typed discriminants so no compose event
    rides untyped. Land a real-bus cross-pane integration test as the anti-recurrence forcing function
    (evidence the receivers are mounted + reachable, per the project E2E definition-of-done row 3).
    This task supersedes and absorbs task 070.
  </prompt>

  <role>
    Frontend developer fluent in the SpaarkeAi three-pane shell (ThreePaneShell, ContextPaneController,
    ConversationPane), the PaneEventBus contract (@spaarke/events-components) and its compose-contracts.ts
    discriminants, and the ComposeWorkspace/ComposeEditor emit sites. Comfortable writing a real-bus jest
    integration test that drives a compose event across panes.
  </role>

  <goal>
    Emitting each of the six compose flow signals on the PaneEventBus produces the correct cross-pane
    effect: the Context pane and the Assistant/Conversation pane react on their OWN controllers (not a
    log-only handler on ComposeWorkspace). Flows 3, 4, and 6 are built (not stubs). All six `compose_*`
    signals are typed discriminants in the bus union — zero `as any`. A real-PaneEventBus jest test drives
    each flow emit → asserts the receiving pane's observable effect. Cluster 5 gaps 5.1–5.2 closed; task
    070 folded in and marked superseded.
  </goal>

